well we're talking with eric palacios  about evictions during the pandemic a  lot of people have struggled  to pay rent and there has been a  moratorium on evictions and here's more  on that  eric there is a moratorium right now  on addictions but that's not going to  last a whole lot longer when are we  going to start seeing  evictions well the governor made an  announcement uh last week that he is  going to extend the moratorium until the  end of march  so if you are in danger of uh being  evicted of losing  uh your rental space your home uh  because of non-payment of rent uh you're  protected  at least until the end of march uh is  there any reason a landlord could  evict a tenant before then yes the  moratorium is not all-inclusive the  moratorium is only for  non-payment of rent so if your contract  is up if you had a one-year contract and  that has expired in your month to month  your landlord can choose not to continue  the contract if you violate any clause  in the lease if you have pets that were  not  stipulated or not allowed if you had  more people on the property if you're  conducting a business that you shouldn't  be conducting in the property or if you  violate  any law you can be evicted for cause for  those reasons  so what advice do you have then for both  landlords and for tenants  well it's important to to know that this  is not a rent forgiveness  if you are haven't been able to work if  you can't make your rent payment  it doesn't mean that this debt is going  away you still owe it  you simply need to make arrangements  with your landlord to pay it back  as soon as possible if you're a landlord  and your tenant isn't paying  the rent then it's important to  communicate with them and to see why  this is happening and if there's any  type of uh arrangement or agreement that  can be reached between the two of you  yeah so if a lieutenant has not been  able to pay rent  uh for some time now that debt is just  accumulating right  that's correct it's accumulating uh the  interest and  penalties might be waived in the future  uh  by a judge but the rent is still going  to be due  so it's important to keep this in mind  you don't want to take advantage of the  system if you're still working if you're  still able to  make the payment don't take advantage of  the situation and not pay rent  it's going to bite you in the end you're  going to have to pay it  as always great information eric really  appreciate you joining us  thanks for having me take care for more  information you can give eric palacios  and associates a call that phone number  their website 702-444-7777  ericpalacioslaw.com
 

